### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [x] I have searched the existing issues

### Describe the bug

`RendererSynchronizationContext` can be left installed on a thread-pool thread that does not own the renderer. Because `RendererSynchronizationContextDispatcher.CheckAccess()` is nothing but

```csharp
public override bool CheckAccess() => SynchronizationContext.Current == _context;
```

that thread then passes every access check for the rest of its work item: `Dispatcher.AssertAccess()` succeeds, and `Dispatcher.InvokeAsync(...)` runs the work item inline instead of queueing it. Two threads can therefore render the same component tree at the same time.

The leak needs three things to line up:

1. A thread calls `InvokeAsync(Func<Task>)`, `InvokeAsync(Func<Task<T>>)` or `Send` while the renderer is idle, so the work runs inline on that thread. Call this thread the holder.
2. While the holder is inside, any other thread submits work with the execution context suppressed (`ExecutionContext.SuppressFlow()`), or otherwise without a captured `ExecutionContext`. Any submitting entry point will do, including `Post`.
3. The holder keeps doing unrelated work on that thread after the call returns.

The leak lands on the holder's thread, not on the thread that suppressed flow. See "Which calls are affected" below for the measured matrix.

None of the three is unusual, and none is misuse of the API. Condition 2 is the one that most callers never hit, which is presumably why this has gone unnoticed: a continuation that carries an `ExecutionContext` gets its `SynchronizationContext` restored by `ExecutionContext.RunInternal` on the way out, and that restore is what normally hides the bug.

The repro below uses public API only (`Dispatcher.CreateDefault()`) and reproduces deterministically, 10 runs out of 10, on 8.0.31, 9.0.20, 10.0.10 and 11.0.0-rc.1.

### Expected Behavior

After `Dispatcher.InvokeAsync(...)` returns to a caller that was not already inside the dispatcher, `Dispatcher.CheckAccess()` on that caller's thread should be `false`, and work submitted from that thread should go through the queue.

### Steps To Reproduce

Single file, `Microsoft.NET.Sdk` with `<FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />`. Exits 1 on every version I tried.

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;

var dispatcher = Dispatcher.CreateDefault();
var rendererEntered = new ManualResetEventSlim();
var workItemQueued = new ManualResetEventSlim();
var callerDone = new ManualResetEventSlim();
var leaked = false;

// Thread P: the dispatcher is idle, so this work item runs inline on P and P gets
// the renderer's SynchronizationContext for the duration of the call.
ThreadPool.UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem(_ =>
{
    dispatcher.InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        rendererEntered.Set();
        workItemQueued.Wait();
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    });

    // Back in P's own work item, outside of any dispatcher call.
    leaked = dispatcher.CheckAccess();
    callerDone.Set();
}, null);

rendererEntered.Wait();

// Thread Q: the dispatcher is busy, so this is queued behind P's work item.
// Under SuppressFlow() the continuation that dequeues it captures no ExecutionContext.
using (ExecutionContext.SuppressFlow())
    _ = dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => { });

workItemQueued.Set();
callerDone.Wait();

Console.WriteLine($".NET {Environment.Version}");
Console.WriteLine($"CheckAccess() on an unrelated thread-pool thread: {leaked} (expected False)");
return leaked ? 1 : 0;
```

```
.NET 10.0.10
CheckAccess() on an unrelated thread-pool thread: True (expected False)
```

#### As tests

These are written against `Dispatcher.CreateDefault()` so they run anywhere; inside `src/Components/Components/test/Rendering/RendererSynchronizationContextTest.cs` you would construct `new RendererSynchronizationContext()` instead and compare `SynchronizationContext.Current` to it. The first two fail today, the third passes and is there to isolate the cause.

```csharp
public TimeSpan Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);

[Fact]
public void InvokeAsync_DoesNotLeaveSyncContextOnCallerThread_WhenWorkIsQueuedWithSuppressedFlow()
{
    // Arrange
    var dispatcher = Dispatcher.CreateDefault();
    var rendererEntered = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var workItemQueued = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var callerDone = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var leaked = false;

    // Act
    // The dispatcher is idle, so this work item runs inline on the thread-pool
    // thread, which gets the renderer's SynchronizationContext for the duration.
    ThreadPool.UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem(_ =>
    {
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            rendererEntered.Set();
            workItemQueued.Wait(Timeout);
            await Task.CompletedTask;
        });

        // Back in this thread's own work item, outside of any dispatcher call.
        leaked = dispatcher.CheckAccess();
        callerDone.Set();
    }, null);

    Assert.True(rendererEntered.Wait(Timeout));

    // Another thread queues work while the dispatcher is busy. Its continuation
    // captures no ExecutionContext, so nothing restores the thread's previous
    // SynchronizationContext after it runs.
    using (ExecutionContext.SuppressFlow())
    {
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => { });
    }

    workItemQueued.Set();
    Assert.True(callerDone.Wait(Timeout));

    // Assert
    Assert.False(leaked);
}

[Fact]
public void LeakedSyncContext_DoesNotLetCallerBypassTheQueue()
{
    // Arrange
    var dispatcher = Dispatcher.CreateDefault();
    var rendererEntered = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var workItemQueued = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var leakChecked = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var holderInside = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var holderRelease = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var callerDone = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var overlapped = false;

    // Act
    ThreadPool.UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem(_ =>
    {
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            rendererEntered.Set();
            workItemQueued.Wait(Timeout);
            await Task.CompletedTask;
        });

        leakChecked.Set();

        // Wait until another thread is inside the dispatcher and still holding it,
        // then submit work of our own.
        holderInside.Wait(Timeout);
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => overlapped = holderInside.IsSet && !holderRelease.IsSet);
        holderRelease.Set();
        callerDone.Set();
    }, null);

    Assert.True(rendererEntered.Wait(Timeout));
    using (ExecutionContext.SuppressFlow())
    {
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => { });
    }
    workItemQueued.Set();

    // This thread takes the now-idle dispatcher and holds it.
    Assert.True(leakChecked.Wait(Timeout));
    dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() =>
    {
        holderInside.Set();
        holderRelease.Wait(Timeout);
    }).GetAwaiter().GetResult();

    Assert.True(callerDone.Wait(Timeout));

    // Assert
    Assert.False(overlapped);
}

[Fact]
public void InvokeAsync_DoesNotLeaveSyncContextOnCallerThread_WhenFlowIsNotSuppressed()
{
    // Arrange
    var dispatcher = Dispatcher.CreateDefault();
    var rendererEntered = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var workItemQueued = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var callerDone = new ManualResetEventSlim();
    var leaked = false;

    // Act
    ThreadPool.UnsafeQueueUserWorkItem(_ =>
    {
        dispatcher.InvokeAsync(async () =>
        {
            rendererEntered.Set();
            workItemQueued.Wait(Timeout);
            await Task.CompletedTask;
        });

        leaked = dispatcher.CheckAccess();
        callerDone.Set();
    }, null);

    Assert.True(rendererEntered.Wait(Timeout));
    dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => { });
    workItemQueued.Set();
    Assert.True(callerDone.Wait(Timeout));

    // Assert
    Assert.False(leaked);
}
```

```
[xUnit.net 00:00:00.30]     InvokeAsync_DoesNotLeaveSyncContextOnCallerThread_WhenWorkIsQueuedWithSuppressedFlow [FAIL]
[xUnit.net 00:00:00.31]     LeakedSyncContext_DoesNotLetCallerBypassTheQueue [FAIL]
Failed!  - Failed: 2, Passed: 1, Skipped: 0, Total: 3
```

### Exceptions (if any)

In a Blazor Server app this shows up as two threads rendering one component at once, roughly a millisecond apart, followed by the circuit dying and the browser reloading:

```
Element reference captures may only be added as children of frames of type Element
System.NullReferenceException
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.RenderTree.Renderer.ProcessRenderQueue()
Unhandled exception in circuit '...'
```

The exception is a symptom of the concurrent render, so it varies; what is constant is that two threads are inside the render tree.

### .NET Version

11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128 (SDK). Reproduced on runtimes 8.0.31, 9.0.20, 10.0.10 and 11.0.0-rc.1.

### Anything else?

#### Why the context is left behind

Two separate mechanisms would normally undo the `SetSynchronizationContext(this)` in `PostAsync`, and this sequence defeats both.

The thread-pool dispatch loop resets the thread after each work item, which is what the comment on the line refers to ([ThreadPoolWorkQueue.cs#L1013-L1018](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Threading/ThreadPoolWorkQueue.cs#L1013-L1018)):

https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/ec33757f7346be300c356208b3ab2ab141772c2d/src/Components/Components/src/Rendering/RendererSynchronizationContext.cs#L191-L203

That holds only if the continuation is its own work item. The other mechanism is [`ExecutionContext.RunInternal`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Threading/ExecutionContext.cs#L206-L211), which unconditionally restores the thread's previous `SynchronizationContext` when the continuation returns. Whether a continuation goes through it is decided by whether it captured an `ExecutionContext` ([AsyncTaskMethodBuilderT.cs#L519-L535](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Runtime/CompilerServices/AsyncTaskMethodBuilderT.cs#L519-L535)):

```csharp
ExecutionContext? context = Context;
if (context == ExecutionContext.DefaultFlowSuppressed)
{
    Debug.Assert(StateMachine != null);
    StateMachine.MoveNext();          // nothing is saved, nothing is restored
}
else
{
    if (threadPoolThread is null)
        ExecutionContext.RunInternal(context, s_callback, this);   // restores SynchronizationContext
    ...
```

(In shipped 8.0-10.0 the same branch is spelled `context == null`; `main` uses the `DefaultFlowSuppressed` sentinel. Same meaning: a flow-suppressed continuation skips `RunInternal`.)

So under `SuppressFlow()`, restorer two is gone. Restorer one is then defeated by the ordering in `InvokeWithThisAsCurrentSyncCtxThenSetResult`:

https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/ec33757f7346be300c356208b3ab2ab141772c2d/src/Components/Components/src/Rendering/RendererSynchronizationContext.cs#L234-L250

The context is restored *before* the queue marker is completed. By the time `completion.SetResult()` runs, `SynchronizationContext.Current` on this thread is back to whatever it was (`null` for a pool thread), so [`AwaitTaskContinuation.IsValidLocationForInlining`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Threading/Tasks/TaskContinuation.cs#L618-L630) is `true` and [`RunOrScheduleAction`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Threading/Tasks/TaskContinuation.cs#L774-L804) runs the waiting `PostAsync` continuation inline on this thread rather than queueing it. That inline path saves and restores `t_currentTask` and nothing else, because it delegates context restoration to `MoveNext` - the branch that a null/suppressed `ExecutionContext` skips.

The result is that `SetSynchronizationContext(this)` on line 196 executes nested inside a work item belonging to someone else, and survives until that work item ends.

`ConfigureAwaitOptions.ForceYielding` on line 193 does not prevent this: it only affects [`IsCompleted`](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/99b0c182e093958f4d0e3dd6737b1956998d22de/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Runtime/CompilerServices/TaskAwaiter.cs#L431), so it forces a continuation to be registered, but once the antecedent completes later, that continuation is subject to the usual inlining rules.

#### Which calls are affected

I ran every combination of entry point for the two roles against a verbatim copy of the class. `ok` / `LEAK` is `SynchronizationContext.Current == context` on the holder's thread after its call returned.

Flow suppressed by the submitting thread:

```
  holder \ submitter   Action      Func<T>     Func<Task>  Func<Task<T>>  Post
  Action               ok          ok          ok          ok             ok
  Func<T>              ok          ok          ok          ok             ok
  Func<Task>           LEAK        LEAK        LEAK        LEAK           LEAK
  Func<Task<T>>        LEAK        LEAK        LEAK        LEAK           LEAK
  Send                 LEAK        LEAK        LEAK        LEAK           LEAK
```

With flow suppressed by the holder instead, or by nobody, all 25 combinations are `ok`.

Three things follow:

- The holder's entry point decides whether the bug is reachable. `InvokeAsync(Func<Task>)`, `InvokeAsync(Func<Task<T>>)` and `Send` are affected because all three go through `InvokeWithThisAsCurrentSyncCtxThenSetResult`. `InvokeAsync(Action)` and `InvokeAsync(Func<T>)` are not, because their `Execute` completes the builder while this context is still current and restores afterwards ([L60-L77](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/ec33757f7346be300c356208b3ab2ab141772c2d/src/Components/Components/src/Rendering/RendererSynchronizationContext.cs#L60-L77)).
- The submitting entry point is irrelevant - everything funnels into `PostAsync`, `Post` included. What matters is only that the queue was busy and that no `ExecutionContext` was captured.
- Suppressing flow around the holder's own call is harmless. It has to be the submitting thread, which means a call site can be the victim without doing anything unusual itself.

#### How long the leak lasts

```
  thread-pool thread, next work item:  cleared by the pool's reset
  dedicated thread, later in its life: STILL LEAKED (never reset)
```

On a pool thread the damage is bounded by the current work item, which is what the comment in `PostAsync` is counting on. A holder that is not a pool thread never runs `ExecutionContext.ResetThreadPoolThread`, so it keeps the renderer's `SynchronizationContext` for the rest of its life, and `CheckAccess()` keeps returning `true` on it indefinitely.

#### Candidate fix

Completing the marker before restoring the context makes `IsValidLocationForInlining` `false`, so the continuation is queued as its own work item and the dispatch loop's reset covers it - the same shape the `Action` overload already has:

```csharp
finally
{
    try
    {
        // Complete the task queue marker while this context is still current, so that
        // awaiting continuations are queued rather than inlined onto this thread.
        completion.SetResult();
    }
    finally
    {
        SetSynchronizationContext(original);
    }
}
```

I verified this by running the same checks against a verbatim copy of `RendererSynchronizationContext` and against a copy with only that change:

| Check | Shipping `Dispatcher` | Verbatim copy | Patched copy |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Leaks renderer context to the caller thread | LEAK | LEAK | ok |
| 2. Leaked thread renders concurrently with the renderer | LEAK | LEAK | ok |
| 3. Control: same sequence, flow not suppressed | ok | ok | ok |
| 4. Control: renderer held with `InvokeAsync(Action)` | ok | ok | ok |
| 5. Stress: 8 threads x 200 work items, exclusive, none lost | ok | ok | ok |

Same results on net8.0, net9.0, net10.0 and net11.0. I have not run the existing `RendererSynchronizationContextTest` suite against the change, so please treat check 5 as a smoke test rather than proof that ordering semantics are preserved.

An alternative fix on the caller's side is to post under a non-null but empty `ExecutionContext` (`ExecutionContext.Run(<empty context>, ...)`) so that `RunInternal` restores the thread. That works too, but it only protects callers who know to do it, and it cannot help code that suppresses flow for unrelated reasons.

#### Who runs into this

I found it through [ActualLab.Fusion](https://github.com/ActualLab/Fusion), whose `StatefulComponentBase` notifies components under `ExecutionContext.SuppressFlow()` to keep `AsyncLocal`s out of rendering, and whose state update cycle calls `Dispatcher.InvokeAsync(Func<Task>)` from a timer continuation on a pool thread. That supplies all three conditions on every state change of every component, so a rare race becomes routine: an instrumented run of a production Blazor Server app recorded 84 leaked contexts in 30 seconds, and 2 of 8 sessions ended with a dead circuit. Any code that suppresses execution flow around a `StateHasChanged` notification will hit the same thing.

Fixes #67462

## Overview

`OpenIdConnectHandler` already sent an effective `max_age` from `OpenIdConnectChallengeProperties.MaxAge` or `OpenIdConnectOptions.MaxAge`, but accepted an ID token without checking its signed `auth_time`. The pre-fix end-to-end reproduction sent `max_age=300` with no `auth_time`; authentication still completed with `302 Found` instead of failing.

This change automatically validates `auth_time` whenever an effective `MaxAge` is sent. It adds no public API. A process-wide AppContext compatibility switch, `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect.DisableMaxAgeValidation`, is off by default; explicitly setting it to `true` restores the legacy request-only behavior for temporarily incompatible providers.

## Design

The exact final ordinary authorization-request value is captured after `OnRedirectToIdentityProvider` and stored in protected `AuthenticationProperties` state, so per-challenge values, option fallback, concurrent challenges, and redirect-event mutation cannot cross-talk:

```csharp
// OpenIdConnectHandler.cs:481-485
message = redirectContext.ProtocolMessage;

if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(message.MaxAge))
{
    properties.Items[MaxAgeProperty] = message.MaxAge;
}
```

The callback removes that internal state before ticket construction. Validation runs only after existing signature, issuer, audience, lifetime, nonce, and OIDC protocol validation. Front-channel ID tokens are checked after `ValidateAuthenticationResponse`; token-endpoint ID tokens are checked after `ValidateTokenResponse`, so hybrid flows validate both tokens:

```csharp
// OpenIdConnectHandler.cs:828-831, 927-931
if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(authorizationResponse.IdToken))
{
    ValidateAuthTime(jwt, maxAge);
}

// ... code redemption and token-response protocol validation ...
ValidateAuthTime(tokenEndpointJwt, maxAge);
```

`auth_time` must be an integral, nonnegative OIDC NumericDate representable by `DateTimeOffset`. Using `Options.TimeProvider.GetUtcNow()` and `TokenValidationParameters.ClockSkew`, validation rejects:

- `auth_time > now + skew`
- `now - auth_time > max_age + skew`

Exact boundaries and values within skew are accepted. Missing, string-valued, fractional, negative, overflowed, stale, or excessively future values fail through the existing authentication-failed and remote-failure paths. No automatic rechallenge occurs, and UserInfo retrieval, `SaveTokens`, ticket creation, and sign-in do not continue.

For PAR, changing `max_age` in `OnPushAuthorization` is rejected while validation is active because protected state has already correlated the ordinary redirect value. Mutation remains allowed when push is skipped or the compatibility switch restores legacy request-only behavior. Applications should make effective request changes in `OnRedirectToIdentityProvider`.

## Implementation

- Preserves existing per-challenge-over-options precedence and outgoing serialization.
- Correlates the exact final `max_age` through protected transaction state.
- Supports the default `TokenHandler`/`JsonWebToken` path and legacy `SecurityTokenValidator`/`JwtSecurityToken` path.
- Covers authorization code, implicit (`id_token`, `id_token token`), and hybrid (`code token`, `code id_token`, `code id_token token`) paths.
- Validates both front- and back-channel ID tokens in hybrid flows.
- Leaves behavior unchanged when `MaxAge` is unset.
- Documents the changed `MaxAge` contract, clock-skew behavior, PAR constraint, compatibility switch, and trust boundary in XML docs and `PACKAGE.md`.

The compatibility switch can be enabled in runtime configuration:

```json
{
  "runtimeOptions": {
    "configProperties": {
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect.DisableMaxAgeValidation": true
    }
  }
}
```

or before authentication starts:

```csharp
AppContext.SetSwitch(
    "Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.OpenIdConnect.DisableMaxAgeValidation",
    true);
```

The switch defaults to `false` and is intended only as a temporary compatibility escape hatch. Only applications already setting `MaxAge` are affected. The behavioral compatibility announcement is [aspnet/Announcements#537](https://github.com/aspnet/Announcements/issues/537).

## Outcome

- **Red reproduction on `origin/main`:** missing `auth_time` with `max_age=300` completed authentication (`302 Found`) instead of the expected failure (`400 Bad Request`).
- **Focused green coverage:** 41/41 cases passed, including options/per-challenge precedence, protected correlation and concurrent isolation, exact/stale/future/skew boundaries, invalid NumericDates, every supported flow shape, both token handlers, event ordering, downstream termination, PAR behavior, and switch unset/false/true behavior.
- **Full affected project:** `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Test` passed **868**, failed **0**, skipped **3** (pre-existing skips), total **871**.
- **Formatting/diff:** focused `dotnet format` completed and `git diff --check` passed.
- **Independent ASP.NET Core review:** identified that the initial PAR mutation guard also ran when validation was disabled or push was skipped. The guard is now conditional on active validation and an actually used pushed request, with regression coverage for both cases.
- **Compatibility:** aspnet/Announcements#537 documents the behavioral change, affected applications, exact failure conditions, trust boundary, and temporary switch.
- **Public API:** none added or changed; no `PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt` update.

The guarantee is deliberately narrow: ASP.NET Core validates the trusted identity provider's signed `auth_time` assertion against the exact `max_age` it sent. It cannot prove the physical authentication ceremony or protect against a compromised identity provider that signs false data. UserInfo, refresh-token redemption, local cookie lifetime, and physical user interaction are not revalidated.

## Overview

This branch centralizes the existing Antiforgery claim-UID algorithm as an internal shared-source `ClaimsPrincipal` identifier, then adopts it in Antiforgery, MVC Cache Tag Helper (including distributed caching), and Components `CacheView`. The hard constraint is **byte-for-byte Antiforgery compatibility**: identity/claim traversal, UTF-8 framing, sorting, and SHA-256 output remain unchanged so deployed tokens stay valid. No public API signatures change; Output Caching and Response Caching behavior remain unchanged.

The complete change comprises ten commits (`5002ac85f14e` through `423f6a47ed`) and 16 files. CacheView user-varied keys intentionally turn over. Cache Tag Helper now unconditionally versions its internal key format, so all existing memory/distributed entries become misses and expire normally rather than risking overlap with the former unescaped `Identity.Name` suffix.

## Design

The shared primitive is internal source, compiled into each consumer assembly:

```csharp
// src/Shared/SecurityHelper/SecurityHelper.cs
// Callers provide the SHA-256 destination; no result array is allocated.
internal const int UserIdentifierSize = SHA256.HashSizeInBytes;

public static bool TryGetUserIdentifier(
    ClaimsPrincipal? principal,
    Span<byte> destination);
```

The identifier contract, documented on the existing Cache Tag Helper and CacheView `VaryByUser` properties, is:

- Traverse authenticated identities in principal order.
- Within each identity, check exact-ordinal claim types in precedence order: `sub`, `ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier`, then `ClaimTypes.Upn`; hash the selected claim's ordinal `(type, value, issuer)` tuple.
- If no recognized claim is available, collect every claim from authenticated identities, sort by claim type exactly as Antiforgery did, and hash every `(type, value, issuer)` tuple. Equal-type fallback order intentionally retains the previous runtime sort behavior.
- Return `false` for a null principal or when authenticated identities contribute no claims.
- Applications must provide trusted, unique, stable claim tuples and issuers; identity and relevant claim ordering must be deterministic, and claims transformations must run before key generation.

Authentication type and `Identity.Name` are not part of the shared identifier. Cache consumers separately domain claimless authenticated principals from anonymous principals because content can depend on `IsAuthenticated`. Antiforgery's later `Identity.Name` fallback remains Antiforgery-specific.

Cache Tag Helper keeps the existing `VaryByUser` property and `vary-by-user` attribute. Versioning is an unconditional private cache-key token, not a new parameter: keys begin `<CacheTagHelper-or-DistributedCacheTagHelper>||v1||<key>`, and user variance still uses `VaryByUser`. The public `CacheView`, `CacheTagHelper`, and `DistributedCacheTagHelper` type documentation now states consistently that generated keys are framework implementation details, may change between ASP.NET Core product versions, and can cause older entries to become misses and expire normally; it does not expose a stable key format or promise migration.

Output Caching continues to reject authenticated/Authorization requests under its default policy, with custom `VaryByValue` as explicit opt-in; Response Caching continues to follow HTTP shared-cache rules. Neither subsystem gains principal-based variance or a new API.

## Implementation

The common path scans standard list-backed principals and identities as spans and returns as soon as it finds the first recognized claim. Only unusual identity enumerables, the all-claims fallback, or serialized inputs over 256 bytes use pooled buffers:

```csharp
// src/Shared/SecurityHelper/SecurityHelper.cs
private static bool TryGetUserIdentifier(
    ReadOnlySpan<ClaimsIdentity> identities,
    Span<byte> destination)
{
    for (var i = 0; i < identities.Length; i++)
    {
        var identity = identities[i];
        if (!identity.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            continue;
        }

        var identifierClaim = FindUserIdentifierClaim(identity);
        if (identifierClaim is not null)
        {
            ComputeSha256(identifierClaim, destination);
            return true; // common well-known-claim path: no pool rental
        }
    }

    // No recognized claim: rent, collect authenticated claims, sort by ordinal type, and hash.
    Claim[] rentedClaims = ArrayPool<Claim>.Shared.Rent(InitialPoolSize);
    // (collection/growth omitted)
    var claims = rentedClaims.AsSpan(0, claimCount);
    claims.Sort(static (a, b) => string.Compare(a.Type, b.Type, StringComparison.Ordinal));
    ComputeSha256(claims, destination);
    return true;
}
```

Exact `ClaimsIdentity` instances use the direct scan; derived identities preserve virtual `FindFirst(Predicate<Claim>)` dispatch because changing an override's result could invalidate existing Antiforgery tokens. Hash input preserves `BinaryWriter.Write(string)` framing: UTF-8 bytes preceded by a 7-bit encoded byte length. For example, byte length 200 is encoded as `0xC8, 0x01` before the payload:

```csharp
// src/Shared/SecurityHelper/SecurityHelper.cs
var buffer = totalSize <= StackAllocThreshold
    ? stackalloc byte[StackAllocThreshold]
    : (rentedBuffer = ArrayPool<byte>.Shared.Rent(totalSize));

var offset = Write7BitEncodedString(buffer, claim.Type);
offset += Write7BitEncodedString(buffer[offset..], claim.Value);
offset += Write7BitEncodedString(buffer[offset..], claim.Issuer);
SHA256.HashData(buffer[..offset], destination);
```

Antiforgery retains its service/interface boundary and delegates only extraction:

```csharp
// src/Antiforgery/src/Internal/DefaultClaimUidExtractor.cs
public bool TryExtractClaimUidBytes(ClaimsPrincipal claimsPrincipal, Span<byte> destination)
{
    Debug.Assert(claimsPrincipal != null);
    return SecurityHelper.TryGetUserIdentifier(claimsPrincipal, destination);
}
```

CacheView appends one of three disjoint domains inside its enclosing SHA-256 key:

```csharp
// src/Components/Endpoints/src/CacheView/CacheViewKeyResolver.cs
Span<byte> userIdentifier = stackalloc byte[SecurityHelper.UserIdentifierSize];
if (!SecurityHelper.TryGetUserIdentifier(user, userIdentifier))
{
    AppendLengthPrefixedString(
        hash,
        SecurityHelper.IsAuthenticated(user) ? "AuthenticatedWithoutIdentifier" : "Anonymous");
    return;
}

AppendLengthPrefixedString(hash, "Identifier");
hash.AppendData(userIdentifier);
```

MVC Cache Tag Helper uses the same three states in both equality and generated text. Its format version is unconditional and the existing user token is unchanged:

```csharp
// src/Mvc/Mvc.TagHelpers/src/Cache/CacheTagKey.cs
var builder = new StringBuilder(_prefix);
builder
    .Append(CacheKeyTokenSeparator)
    .Append(CacheKeyVersion) // "v1" for every key, regardless of vary options
    .Append(CacheKeyTokenSeparator)
    .Append(Key);

// Later, only when VaryByUser is enabled:
builder
    .Append(CacheKeyTokenSeparator)
    .Append(VaryByUserName) // still "VaryByUser"
    .Append(CacheKeyTokenSeparator)
    .Append(_userIdentifierName);
```

The version token prevents a legacy entry for the same logical key—`...||VaryByUser||<unescaped Identity.Name>`—from matching the new format. Without a versioned domain, an old name `NoIdentifier` exactly matched the initial new anonymous suffix, and `Identifier||<digest>` could match an identified suffix. The prerequisite is narrow (a surviving distributed entry and matching old name), but the consequence is cross-user cached-content reuse.

